1. `#!/bin/bash` – Tells the system to execute the script using the Bash shell.
2. `$#` – Gives the total number of arguments passed to the script.
3. `-ne` – Checks whether two values are not equal.
4. `if` – Checks a given condition.
5. `then` – Executes commands when the condition is true.
6. `echo` – Displays a message on the screen.
7. `exit 1` – Terminates the script with an error status.
8. `$1` – Stores the filename passed as the first argument.
9. `$2` – Stores the starting line number passed as the second argument.
10. `$3` – Stores the ending line number passed as the third argument.
11. `sed` – Used to search, edit and display text from a file.
12. `-n` – Prevents automatic printing of lines.
13. `p` – Prints the selected lines.
14. `"${start},${end}p"` – Specifies the range of lines to be displayed, from the starting line to the ending line.
